        "problem": "The quartic polynomial $P(x)$ has roots ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=-2,$ ${x}_{3}=-4,$ and ${x}_{4}=4,$ and the coefficient of the quartic term is $9.$ Determine the **expanded form** of $P(x).$\n\n$P(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$-\\,576$",
        "worked_solution": "Since our quartic polynomial has roots ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=-2,$ ${x}_{3}=-4,$ and ${x}_{4}=4,$ and the coefficient of the quartic term is $a=9,$ we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2})(x-{x}_{3})(x-{x}_{4}) & =9(x-(-2))(x-(-2))(x-(-4))(x-4) \\\\  & =9(x+2)(x+2)(x+4)(x-4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9(x+2)(x+2)(x+4)(x-4) & =9({x}^{2}+4x+4)({x}^{2}-16) \\\\  & =9({x}^{4}-16{x}^{2}+4{x}^{3}-64x+4{x}^{2}-64) \\\\  & =9({x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2}-64x-64) \\\\  & =9{x}^{4}+36{x}^{3}-108{x}^{2}-576x-576.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=9{x}^{4}+36{x}^{3}-108{x}^{2}-576x-576.$",
